'åU4Ä|œÀTŽöb	¢„úH3ÉŒŸIF±"ÛóêbÅ{"url":"/gcsl/language/deferred-work/","fragment":{"page_number":0,"data":{"url":"/gcsl/language/deferred-work/","content":"Deferred Work. A future represents a calculation whose result is needed later. lazy creates that calculation without running it. Awaiting it evaluates the body and remembers the successful result. Futures are deferred work, not concurrent tasks. This program creates one future and then reads its result: Copylet answer = lazy 21 * 2 await answer Where work happens # A lazy body is an impure context, even when a pure function creates it. That lets a pure function plan effects without performing them. A pure function cannot await a future. Resolve the value in an impure caller before passing it into a pure calculation that needs the value itself. The checker inserts implicit awaits at uses that need a value's contents, such as arithmetic or a required present argument. It applies the same purity restriction to explicit and implicit awaits. A call to a pure function never secretly starts deferred work inside that function. Writing contracts # Use explicit wrappers when the distinction is part of an interface: TypeContract Present<Int> A present integer, equivalent to Int in an ordinary annotation. Future<Int> Deferred work yielding an integer. `Int. Future ` A present value does not satisfy an explicitly required Future<T>. Nested future layers collapse: awaiting a future resolves it fully. Awaiting an already present value is a silent no-op. Bare types in impure parameters accept present or deferred values, including bare positions inside records and lists. Pure parameters infer whether their bodies need the value's contents. Impure and memoized results infer their present-or-future shape from the body. Explicit wrappers keep the contract you wrote. Avoiding surprising inference # A pure function with result Int requires a present result, even if it only returns its argument. It does not preserve a future's shape polymorphically. Recursive result inference also retains conservative parts of the declared signature. Use Present<T> or an explicit future contract where an API needs a stable distinction, and consult Inference Limits when an inferred type is wider than expected.","word_count":329,"filters":{},"meta":{"crumbs":"GCSL / The Language","title":"Deferred Work"},"anchors":[{"element":"h2","id":"where-work-happens","text":"Where work happens #","location":55},{"element":"h2","id":"writing-contracts","text":"Writing contracts #","location":154},{"element":"h2","id":"avoiding-surprising-inference","text":"Avoiding surprising inference #","location":263}]}},"word_data":{"part":[{"position":164,"weight":24,"original_word":null},{"position":298,"weight":24,"original_word":null}],"includ":[{"position":228,"weight":24,"original_word":null}],"evalu":[{"position":21,"weight":24,"original_word":null}],"paramet":[{"position":222,"weight":24,"original_word":null},{"position":236,"weight":24,"original_word":null}],"creat":[{"position":13,"weight":24,"original_word":null},{"position":38,"weight":24,"original_word":null},{"position":71,"weight":24,"original_word":null}],"whether":[{"position":238,"weight":24,"original_word":null}],"record":[{"position":232,"weight":24,"original_word":null}],"their":[{"position":239,"weight":24,"original_word":null},{"position":250,"weight":24,"original_word":null}],"puriti":[{"position":133,"weight":24,"original_word":null}],"nest":[{"position":199,"weight":24,"original_word":null}],"checker":[{"position":110,"weight":24,"original_word":null}],"noop":[{"position":217,"weight":24,"original_word":null}],"itself":[{"position":108,"weight":24,"original_word":null}],"accept":[{"position":223,"weight":24,"original_word":null}],"preserv":[{"position":287,"weight":24,"original_word":null}],"repres":[{"position":4,"weight":24,"original_word":null}],"arithmet":[{"position":123,"weight":24,"original_word":null}],"explicit":[{"position":136,"weight":24,"original_word